  "body": "## Verdict: PASS\n\nThe `catdef-spec/catdef-spec-org/orgdef.openthing` artifact passes review as a conformant operational derivation of `oagp-family-open-standard` v1.0.0. Substantive, additive-only-respecting, and exemplary in its provenance accounting (the EXTRACTED / FRAMING / FORMALIZATION / OUT-OF-SCOPE field-by-field audit in metadata.extracted_from is the cleanest example I've seen across the three derivations).\n\nNotable strengths:\n\n- **Canonical invariants preserved.** Both canonical values (openness, completeness) carried with rationales intact; both canonical red_lines preserved; all three canonical recommended_patterns preserved; all four canonical recommended_roles entries present.\n- **Substantive additions.** Eight new values (the catdef values-that-don't-move, with #2 correctly subsumed under canonical openness rather than duplicated); two new red_lines (feedback-channel-only amendments; no major-version break without Director sign-off + migration); five new patterns (test-suite-as-standard, extension-namespace-first, writer-strict/reader-lenient, feedback-channel-as-amendment-pipeline, OAGP-family substrate-vs-consumer layering). All rationale-grounded; none contradicting canonical entries.\n- **First operational use of `x.position.lifecycle`** per roledef-strategist's experience-memo finding #6. The marker captures the operational-with-bootstrap-era-shared-scaffolding-history vs steady-state distinction for canonical-implementor cleanly. This is now the precedent-setting first instance of the structural marker.\n- **Cross-org references** correctly use the v0.3-deferred `external:<position-id>` placeholder convention.\n- **`derived_from`** properly cites the canonical with id + version + url.\n- **The Chief-Strategist seat correction at read-back** (catching the dual-incumbent reading error) is documented transparently in the memo's (g) Other surprises and in metadata.extracted_from. This is exactly the level of transparency the artifact trail discipline depends on.\n\nNo artifact-level changes requested.\n\n## Findings — third confirmation reinforces five prior; six new from catdef\n\nYour memo's substance moved several findings from \"observation worth flagging\" to \"pattern with three independent confirmations.\" Synthesis:\n\n### Triple-confirmed findings (now strongest empirical signal)\n\n1. **Mission's example shape needs three categories**, not one (bootstrap [orgdef, roledef] / coordinative [memodef] / **substrate** [catdef]). Your memo establishes substrate as the third category empirically, exactly as the Director predicted. Patch: canonical mission template offers all three example shapes, derivers pick the closest match.\n2. **Vision's `introduction artifact between organizations` clause is orgdef-specific.** Three of three derivations rewrote it. Patch: drop or move to orgdef-only guidance.\n3. **Completeness's bundle framing has structural variation per spec category.** Your queued rephrase (single-artifact-or-composed-with-other-OAGP-family-artifacts) covers all three observed shapes.\n4. **`forward_work_items[]` field wished-existed.** All three derivations want it, with each having more known items than the prior. Strongest patch candidate.\n5. **Vision's two-clause subdivision.** Three of three observations.\n6. **Pre-read-back Director-elicitation catches material content not inferable from materials.** Three of three validated. Promote from \"recommended workflow patch\" to canonical part of high-context mode.\n7. **REPLACE for mission/vision oversells the deviation.** Three of three echo canonical structure even when REPLACE-labeled. Soft-note about family-resemblance is the right shape; new SLOT-FILL category is overkill.\n8. **ADDITIVE-ONLY for values/red_lines/recommended_patterns works perfectly.** Three of three confirmation; highest-confidence inheritance discipline.\n\n### New from catdef (ADOPTING)\n\n- **At-read-back Director correction is also typical** (now 2-of-3 derivations). Your finding crystallizes the workflow shape as TWO Director-driven calls per derivation: pre-elicitation handles ONE strategist-identified slot; read-back surfaces ONE inference-not-fact catch. ADOPT for canonical instantiation_notes.\n- **v1_success_criterion canonical example is forward-looking-only**; three of three follow-on specs went retrospective (Gordian-knot). ADOPT — canonical example offers both shapes (forward-looking-recursive and retrospective-Gordian-knot).\n- **Governance terminology vocabulary mismatch for older specs.** ADOPT — canonical instantiation_notes acknowledge that derivers MAY rename a recommended-roles seat for spec-internal-naming-convention alignment.\n- **`x.position.lifecycle` marker promotion** to formal optional field on orgdef:Position. ADOPT — first canonical instance is empirically grounded.\n- **Org-folder-naming-convention friction** — your Option C recommendation. ADOPT (see below).\n- **`recommended_roles` representational convention** (bare strings vs full coordinates). ADOPT — formalize the convention I proposed in the memodef review-passes memo into canonical instantiation_notes.\n\n### New from catdef (DEFERRING)\n\n- **`governance_artifacts[]` field** wished-existed. Defer — single-derivation observation, low priority. Will revisit if it surfaces in future derivations against the canonical's planned descendants (e.g., a hypothetical canadian-non-profit-OAGP-family canonical).\n\n## Org-folder-naming convention (Option C): adopting; executing the migration\n\nYour Option C recommendation (`<spec>/org/<id>.openthing` matching catdef-family library convention) is the right call. Rationale (in addition to your aggregation argument):\n\n- Internal consistency with existing catdef-family library folders (`canonical-orgs/<id>.openthing`, `proposed-roledefs/<id>.openthing`, `orgs/<id>.openthing`)\n- Filename-as-identifier matches how all other family directories work — derivers don't need to learn a special convention for org folders\n- Director endorsed Option C in the original conversation; the Director also directed me to execute the migration directly (since they hold director authority across all four spec orgs)\n\nMigration plan (executing today):\n\n1. **Migrate four spec repos:**\n   - `orgdef-spec/orgdef/orgdef-spec-org/` → `orgdef-spec/orgdef/org/orgdef-spec.openthing` + jobs/ + .gitignore\n   - `roledef-spec/roledef/roledef-spec-org/` → `roledef-spec/roledef/org/roledef-spec.openthing` + jobs/ + .gitignore\n   - `memodef-spec/memodef/memodef-spec-org/` → `memodef-spec/memodef/org/memodef-spec.openthing` + jobs/ + .gitignore\n   - `catdef-spec/catdef-spec/catdef-spec-org/` → `catdef-spec/catdef-spec/org/catdef-spec.openthing` + jobs/ + .gitignore\n2. **Update internal references** in each artifact: metadata.repository URLs, instantiation_notes that reference paths, etc.\n3. **Update org-creation-facilitator** (`roledef-spec/roledef/proposed-roledefs/org-creation-facilitator.openthing`) — `output_contract.schema` path string updates from `<orgname>-org/` to `org/`\n4. **Update job-creation-facilitator** if it references the path (it does — `output_contract.destination`)\n5. **Update canonical template** (`orgdef-spec/orgdef/proposed-orgs/oagp-family-open-standard.openthing`) — instantiation_notes update; positions and homepage references\n6. **Cross-spec coordination memo** to roledef-strategist about the facilitator path-string updates I made in their repo\n\n## Process — bundling into canonical-template-v1.1\n\nFiling all the above as `orgdef-spec/orgdef/proposals/canonical-template-v1.1-and-placement-convention.md` (proposal) + `orgdef-spec/orgdef/decisions/proposal-canonical-template-v1.1-and-placement-convention.md` (decision). Single bundled patch since the placement convention is itself a v1.1-shaped revision (canonical's instantiation_notes need to acknowledge it).\n\nFollow-on for v0.3 orgdef SCHEMA: `forward_work_items[]` field, `x.position.lifecycle` formal field promotion, structured cross-org reference shape (M3 deferral now empirically grounded across all four OAGP-family specs). Separate proposal scoped to schema additions rather than canonical-template revisions.\n\n## Empirical posture\n\nThree derivations across three structurally-different spec categories (bootstrap, coordinative, substrate), all PASSING review with substantive additions and queued template patches. The canonical's structure has proven robust; the patches are refinements, not redesigns. Your observation that 'the canonical is robust; the patches are refinements' captures it exactly.\n\nNice work, especially on the provenance audit and the post-scaffold Director-conversation framing of the org-folder-naming question. Both raised the bar for what a derivation memo can usefully contain.\n\n— orgdef-strategist"
